1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,920
Okay. So, you walk into a pawn shop,

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:06,920
glass cases full of gold chains, a wall

3
00:00:04,920 --> 00:00:08,480
of guitars nobody's playing, a couple of

4
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:10,360
power tools sitting on a shelf behind

5
00:00:08,480 --> 00:00:12,640
the counter. It looks like a second-hand

6
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:15,040
store. It looks like somebody's dusty

7
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:18,040
garage sale that somehow got a lease and

8
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:19,800
a neon sign. And if that's all you see,

9
00:00:18,040 --> 00:00:21,800
you're looking at it exactly the way the

10
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:24,080
industry wants you to. Here's what the

11
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:26,040
glass case doesn't show you. A person

12
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:29,280
walks in with a gold ring and the shop

13
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:31,880
gives them $200. That's a loan, not a

14
00:00:29,280 --> 00:00:33,520
sale. The ring sits in the back. And in

15
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:35,920
the state of Georgia, the shop is

16
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:38,000
legally allowed to charge 25% interest

17
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:40,040
on that loan every single month. So,

18
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,280
that person comes back 30 days later and

19
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:44,040
pays $50 just to keep the loan alive for

20
00:00:42,280 --> 00:00:46,120
another month. They haven't paid down a

21
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:47,920
single dollar of what they owe. They've

22
00:00:46,120 --> 00:00:49,720
just paid rent on their own ring. If

23
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:52,480
they keep rolling that loan for a full

24
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:54,680
year, they've handed the shop $600 in

25
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:58,520
interest and they still owe the original

26
00:00:54,680 --> 00:01:00,320
200. That is a 300% annual interest rate

27
00:00:58,520 --> 00:01:02,720
and it's completely legal. There are

28
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:04,280
roughly 3,855

29
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:06,440
pawn shops operating in the United

30
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:08,360
States right now and together they pull

31
00:01:06,440 --> 00:01:10,360
in an estimated 4 and 1/2 billion

32
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:12,800
dollars a year. That's not 4 and 1/2

33
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:14,120
billion in used guitar sales. The bulk

34
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:16,480
of that money comes from the lending

35
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:18,240
side, from people paying interest month

36
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:20,440
after month on loans they took out

37
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:22,080
because they had no other option. But,

38
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:23,800
here's the detail that everyone misses

39
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:26,400
about pawn shops. The stuff on the

40
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:27,960
shelves isn't the product, you are. And

41
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:30,080
the merchandise that does end up on

42
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:32,760
those shelves tells a story the industry

43
00:01:30,080 --> 00:01:35,120
would really rather you didn't hear. So,

44
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:36,680
what is a pawn shop really? Because it's

45
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:40,240
not what the sign on the building says

46
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:42,160
it is. A pawn shop is a bank. That's it.

47
00:01:40,240 --> 00:01:45,000
The thrift store thing, the flea market

48
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:47,200
thing, the used PlayStation for $80,

49
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:49,600
that's the front of the house. A pawn

50
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:51,800
shop is a short-term lender that happens

51
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:53,720
to have display cases. Think of it like

52
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:56,360
a payday lender that holds your stuff

53
00:01:53,720 --> 00:01:59,320
hostage. You You in something valuable,

54
00:01:56,360 --> 00:02:01,040
a watch, a laptop, a piece of jewelry,

55
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:03,479
and the shop looks at it and makes you

56
00:02:01,040 --> 00:02:05,600
an offer. Not an offer to buy it, an

57
00:02:03,479 --> 00:02:07,680
offer to lend you money against it. The

58
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:10,039
item is the collateral, and the loan is

59
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:12,080
the product. You walk out with cash.

60
00:02:10,039 --> 00:02:13,680
Your stuff stays behind the counter, and

61
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:15,520
the only way you get it back is by

62
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:17,680
paying the loan plus whatever interest

63
00:02:15,520 --> 00:02:19,360
has piled up on top of it. Now, here's

64
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:21,000
the part that makes this different from

65
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:23,840
every other kind of lending you've ever

66
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:25,600
seen. A bank wants to know your income.

67
00:02:23,840 --> 00:02:27,600
A credit card company wants to check

68
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:30,280
your credit score. A mortgage lender

69
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:31,840
wants to see 2 years of tax returns. A

70
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:33,520
pawn shop doesn't care about any of

71
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:35,720
that. It doesn't pull your credit

72
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:37,800
history. It doesn't ask where you work

73
00:02:35,720 --> 00:02:39,240
or how much you make. It doesn't even

74
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:41,720
check whether you can afford the

75
00:02:39,240 --> 00:02:43,959
payments because it doesn't need to. The

76
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:46,360
collateral is the safety net. If you

77
00:02:43,959 --> 00:02:49,120
pay, great. They made money on the

78
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:50,640
interest. If you don't pay, great. They

79
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:52,640
keep your stuff and sell it for more

80
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:55,040
than they lent you. The typical pawn

81
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:56,720
loan is somewhere between 25%

82
00:02:55,040 --> 00:02:58,959
of what the shop thinks it can resell

83
00:02:56,720 --> 00:03:00,760
the item for. So, if you bring in a

84
00:02:58,959 --> 00:03:02,840
piece of jewelry the shop could sell for

85
00:03:00,760 --> 00:03:05,800
a thousand dollars, they might lend you

86
00:03:02,840 --> 00:03:08,239
300 or 400 against it. If you never come

87
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:10,440
back, the shop now owns an asset with

88
00:03:08,239 --> 00:03:12,720
$600 of built-in equity sitting right

89
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:14,640
there. They paid 400 for something worth

90
00:03:12,720 --> 00:03:16,840
a thousand. That's a margin most

91
00:03:14,640 --> 00:03:18,959
retailers would kill for, and the shop

92
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:20,920
didn't even have to go shopping. The

93
00:03:18,959 --> 00:03:23,000
shop's ideal customer isn't someone who

94
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:25,040
pays back the loan once and walks away

95
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,680
with their stuff. The ideal customer is

96
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:28,840
someone who keeps rolling the loan,

97
00:03:26,680 --> 00:03:30,480
paying interest every month, unable to

98
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:32,680
afford the full redemption, but too

99
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:34,720
attached to the item to let it go. Pay

100
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:37,400
attention to that because it explains

101
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:38,360
how the entire model actually works.

102
00:03:37,400 --> 00:03:40,239
But, here's where it gets more

103
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:42,519
complicated because there's more than

104
00:03:40,239 --> 00:03:44,200
one way to walk into a pawn shop, and

105
00:03:42,519 --> 00:03:46,239
the shop treats each one very

106
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:48,239
differently. The first is the pawn

107
00:03:46,239 --> 00:03:50,440
transaction. That's the loan we just

108
00:03:48,239 --> 00:03:52,320
talked about. You hand over an item, you

109
00:03:50,440 --> 00:03:54,040
get a loan, and you have a set number of

110
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:55,880
days to come back and redeem it.

111
00:03:54,040 --> 00:03:57,760
Depending on the state, that window is

112
00:03:55,880 --> 00:04:00,080
usually somewhere between 30 and 90

113
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:02,520
days, and some states tack on a grace

114
00:04:00,080 --> 00:04:04,640
period after that. During that time, the

115
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:07,000
shop has to hold your item. They can't

116
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:08,400
sell it, they can't move it, and in most

117
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,160
states, they have to report the

118
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:12,400
transaction electronically to local

119
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:14,400
police, sometimes daily, with a

120
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:17,359
description of the item and a copy of

121
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:19,760
your ID. The system is designed so that

122
00:04:17,359 --> 00:04:21,600
if something turns out to be stolen, law

123
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:24,040
enforcement has a paper trail and a

124
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:26,560
window to catch it. The second way to

125
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:28,480
walk in is the buy transaction. This is

126
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:30,600
an outright sale. You bring in your

127
00:04:28,480 --> 00:04:32,720
item, the shop pays you cash, and they

128
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:35,040
own it right there. No loan, no

129
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:37,440
redemption window, no monthly interest.

130
00:04:35,040 --> 00:04:39,480
The item belongs to the shop. Now, the

131
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:41,160
regulations on buy transactions are

132
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:43,440
different, and this is where it starts

133
00:04:41,160 --> 00:04:45,160
to get interesting. In some states, the

134
00:04:43,440 --> 00:04:47,680
holding period still applies to

135
00:04:45,160 --> 00:04:50,080
purchased items, usually 30 days before

136
00:04:47,680 --> 00:04:51,800
the shop can resell. In others, the

137
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:53,640
rules are looser, the reporting

138
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:55,680
requirements are lighter, and the window

139
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:58,120
between the transaction and any police

140
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:00,880
review is wider. And this is where the

141
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:03,360
gap starts to show. Washington state,

142
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:05,280
for example, has a law that explicitly

143
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:07,960
bans pawn shops from structuring

144
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:10,080
buy-sell transactions to get around pawn

145
00:05:07,960 --> 00:05:12,080
regulations, which tells you something

146
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:14,560
important. It tells you that somebody

147
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:16,120
was doing exactly that, regularly enough

148
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:18,240
and blatantly enough that the state

149
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:20,040
legislature had to write a specific law

150
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:22,040
to stop it. The police reporting is

151
00:05:20,040 --> 00:05:23,360
supposed to be the safety net. Daily

152
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:25,680
electronic submissions of every

153
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:28,120
transaction, descriptions of every item,

154
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:30,760
customer identification on file, and on

155
00:05:28,120 --> 00:05:33,040
paper, that system sounds airtight.

156
00:05:30,760 --> 00:05:35,360
Every item gets tracked, every customer

157
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:37,800
gets identified, and every transaction

158
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:40,560
goes on the record. But in practice, the

159
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:42,680
review process is exactly as patchy as

160
00:05:40,560 --> 00:05:44,800
it sounds. Police departments running

161
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:47,240
these databases are juggling thousands

162
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:48,960
of entries from dozens of shops, and in

163
00:05:47,240 --> 00:05:51,240
a lot of jurisdictions, the review

164
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:54,080
doesn't happen in real time. It happens

165
00:05:51,240 --> 00:05:55,480
in batches, days, or sometimes weeks

166
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:58,200
after the items have already been

167
00:05:55,480 --> 00:06:00,320
logged. And in the meantime, the shop

168
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:02,920
has possession. If the holding period

169
00:06:00,320 --> 00:06:05,640
expires before anyone reviews the entry,

170
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:08,440
the item is legally clear to sell, melt,

171
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:10,560
or ship to a refiner. That timing gap is

172
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:12,240
what makes the whole thing work. So, the

173
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:14,560
real question is, what happens in the

174
00:06:12,240 --> 00:06:16,400
gap between when the shop takes the item

175
00:06:14,560 --> 00:06:18,760
and when anyone actually reviews that

176
00:06:16,400 --> 00:06:20,760
log? Because that gap is where a lot of

177
00:06:18,760 --> 00:06:22,160
things can disappear. If you're the kind

178
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:24,560
of person who likes knowing how the

179
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:26,080
money actually works, hit subscribe

180
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:29,320
because this is where the numbers get

181
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:31,800
wild. So, the system sounds clean.

182
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:33,720
Report everything, hold everything, wait

183
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:35,760
for police review. But, the interest

184
00:06:33,720 --> 00:06:38,080
rate, the actual price tag on these

185
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:40,560
loans, is where the math starts to look

186
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:42,960
predatory. 40 states in the US have a

187
00:06:40,560 --> 00:06:44,440
monthly interest cap on pawn loans. That

188
00:06:42,960 --> 00:06:46,600
sounds reasonable until you look at

189
00:06:44,440 --> 00:06:49,280
where those caps actually sit. In

190
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:52,000
Illinois, pawn shops can charge up to 3%

191
00:06:49,280 --> 00:06:55,320
a month, which works out to about 36%

192
00:06:52,000 --> 00:06:59,360
annualized. In New York, the cap is 4% a

193
00:06:55,320 --> 00:07:01,919
month, or 48% APR. That already sounds

194
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:06,320
steep. In Georgia, the legal ceiling is

195
00:07:01,919 --> 00:07:08,080
25% per month. That's a 300% APR on a

196
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:10,320
loan that most banks wouldn't even

197
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:13,200
recognize as a financial product. And

198
00:07:10,320 --> 00:07:16,960
the range across all 40 states runs from

199
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:19,520
roughly 1% a month at the low end to 25%

200
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:21,840
at the top, which means annualized rates

201
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:24,800
anywhere from about 13% to well over

202
00:07:21,840 --> 00:07:26,919
300%. All of it legal. All of it

203
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:28,960
disclosed in terms the borrower often

204
00:07:26,919 --> 00:07:30,919
doesn't fully understand. And here's the

205
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:33,080
thing about those rates. Pawn shops

206
00:07:30,919 --> 00:07:35,480
don't advertise them as APRs. They

207
00:07:33,080 --> 00:07:38,160
advertise them as monthly fees. You see

208
00:07:35,480 --> 00:07:40,120
a sign that says 5% per month, and it

209
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:42,360
sounds manageable. It sounds like a

210
00:07:40,120 --> 00:07:45,040
small number. But, when you annualize

211
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:47,400
it, you're looking at 60%. When you see

212
00:07:45,040 --> 00:07:52,480
15% per month, which is legal in a bunch

213
00:07:47,400 --> 00:07:55,680
of states, that's 180% APR. And 25% per

214
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:57,800
month, that 300% number in Georgia,

215
00:07:55,680 --> 00:07:59,920
nobody walking into a pawn shop in a

216
00:07:57,800 --> 00:08:02,040
moment of financial desperation is doing

217
00:07:59,920 --> 00:08:03,800
that math in their head. They're looking

218
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:06,200
at the monthly number, and the monthly

219
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:07,720
number looks survivable. Now, here's

220
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:10,200
where the business model turns into

221
00:08:07,720 --> 00:08:12,520
something closer to a machine. The shop

222
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:14,440
wins no matter what you do. If you pay

223
00:08:12,520 --> 00:08:18,360
back the loan, the shop collects

224
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:20,160
interest at margins between 85 and 95%

225
00:08:18,360 --> 00:08:22,880
because the only real cost of making a

226
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:25,080
pawn loan is the cash the shop put up.

227
00:08:22,880 --> 00:08:27,440
There's no loan officer, no credit

228
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:29,760
check, no underwriting department, no

229
00:08:27,440 --> 00:08:31,760
software subscription, no compliance

230
00:08:29,760 --> 00:08:34,400
team reviewing applications. Just

231
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:36,520
capital deployed and interest collected.

232
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:38,479
If you don't pay, the shop keeps the

233
00:08:36,520 --> 00:08:40,680
item and sells it at retail for roughly

234
00:08:38,479 --> 00:08:43,400
two to three times what it lent you,

235
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:46,600
pulling margins of 35 to 60% on the

236
00:08:43,400 --> 00:08:47,920
sale. Either way, the shop profits.

237
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:51,200
There's no version of this where they

238
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:53,600
lose. And roughly 85% of pawn customers

239
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:55,760
do pay back their loans, which means the

240
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:58,440
primary revenue stream isn't the retail

241
00:08:55,760 --> 00:09:00,680
floor at all. It's the interest. You can

242
00:08:58,440 --> 00:09:03,120
see this in the public filings. First

243
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:05,440
Cash Holdings, the largest pawn operator

244
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:08,520
in the United States, reported its US

245
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:11,720
pawn segment numbers for 2024. Retail

246
00:09:08,520 --> 00:09:14,320
merchandise sales brought in $969

247
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:16,440
million. That's a lot, but pawn loan

248
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:19,280
fees, the interest income, brought in

249
00:09:16,440 --> 00:09:22,320
another $505 million, and that line grew

250
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:24,520
16% year-over-year. A half billion

251
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:26,200
dollars just in interest from people

252
00:09:24,520 --> 00:09:28,640
paying month after month to keep their

253
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:30,600
own stuff. On top of that, wholesale

254
00:09:28,640 --> 00:09:32,640
scrap jewelry sales, which is gold and

255
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:34,800
silver melted down and sold by weight,

256
00:09:32,640 --> 00:09:37,960
added another $93 million,

257
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:40,080
up 20%. EZCorp, the other major public

258
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:42,040
pawn company, shows the same pattern

259
00:09:40,080 --> 00:09:44,840
from a different angle. Pawn service

260
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:47,880
charges made up only about 37% of their

261
00:09:44,840 --> 00:09:50,160
total revenue, but they represented 64%

262
00:09:47,880 --> 00:09:52,240
of their gross profit. The interest is

263
00:09:50,160 --> 00:09:54,200
the smaller revenue line, and it's the

264
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:55,680
more profitable one. That's the kind of

265
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:58,040
number that makes you realize the

266
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:59,840
shelves are almost a side hustle. What

267
00:09:58,040 --> 00:10:01,680
comes next is the part that still

268
